static.solution:
(a) We parametrize the upper-hemisphere by, \mathrm{\Phi }: x=9 \cos\theta \sin\phi , y=9 \sin\theta \sin\phi , z=9 \cos\phi with the parameter domain: \mathcal{D}=\left\lbrace( \theta ,\phi ): 0\le \theta \lt 2\pi , 0\le \phi \lt \frac{\pi }{2}\right\rbrace The outward pointing unit normal is : \mathbf{n} = {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} We compute the dot product \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} on the sphere. On the sphere r=9, hence, \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-r}\,{\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-9} {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} = e^{-9}, which is constant on the hemisphere, so the integral is just \big(e^{-9}\big) (\text{area of the hemisphere})=e^{-9}(2)(\pi 9^2)=162e^{-9}. Of course one can obtain the same result by integrating \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_{\mathcal{D}} \left(\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} \right) \,\rho^2 \sin(\phi) d\phi \, d\theta=\int_0^{2\pi } \int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-9}\, 81 \sin\phi \,d\phi \,d\theta= 162\pi e^{-9}\int_0^{\pi/2} \sin\phi \,d\phi =162\pi e^{-9}\left(- \cos\phi \bigg|_0^{\pi/2}\right) = 162\pi e^{-9} (b) We parametrize the part of the sphere inside the first octant of the sphere by, \mathrm{\Phi }: x=6\cos\theta \sin\phi , y=6\sin\theta \sin\phi , z=6\cos\phi with the parameter domain: \mathcal{D}=\left\lbrace( \theta ,\phi ): 0\le \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2} , 0\le \phi \lt \frac{\pi }{2}\right\rbrace Again the outward pointing unit normal is (as seen above): \mathbf{n} = {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} We compute the dot product \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} on the sphere. On the sphere r=6, hence, \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-r}\,{\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \mathbf{n} = e^{-6} {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} \cdot {\mathbf{e}}_{\mathbf{r}} = e^{-6}.
Again this is constant on the sphere, so the integral is \big(e^{-6}\big)(\text{area of the surface}). One eighth of the sphere lies inside the first octant so the area of our surface is one eighth of the area of the sphere. Thus the integral is \big(e^{-6})(1/8)(4\pi 6^2)= \frac{36}{2}e^{-6}
Of course one can obtain the same result by integrating: \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{S} = \iint_{\mathcal{D}} \left(\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{n} \right) \,\rho^2 \sin(\phi) d\phi \, d\theta=\int_0^{\pi/2} \int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-6}\,36 \sin\phi \,d\phi \,d\theta=\frac{36\pi}{2} e^{-6}\int_0^{\pi/2} \sin\phi \,d\phi = \frac{36\pi}{2} e^{-6}\left(- \cos\phi \bigg|_0^{\pi/2}\right)= \frac{36\pi}{2} e^{-6}
